WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.420
Temporary-file operations fail in ordinary production ways.

2
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:14.120
The directory may be missing, permissions may be wrong, disk may be full, quotas may be exceeded, writes may be partial, or a service may restart in the middle of a workflow.

3
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.680
Failure behavior should preserve predictable state.

4
00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:22.740
If creating a temporary file fails, the operation should report a controlled error.

5
00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:29.080
If writing fails after partial output, the program should close resources and remove partial work where appropriate.

6
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.160
Crash recovery matters because temporary files may survive process termination.

7
00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:42.260
Long-running services should define cleanup-on-startup policies, age-based cleanup rules, and operational visibility for orphaned files.

8
00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.560
Cleanup should be scoped to known application-owned directories.

9
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.320
Disk pressure deserves monitoring.

10
00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:53.780
A growing temporary directory can affect availability, neighboring services, or the host.

11
00:00:53.880 --> 00:01:02.280
Metrics for temp-directory size, file counts, cleanup failures, and quota errors can help operators respond before the issue becomes severe.

12
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:08.960
Logging should help diagnose without exposing sensitive paths or contents unnecessarily.

13
00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:16.180
A safe event may include an error category, component, operation, and safe correlation value.

14
00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:22.120
It should avoid dumping file contents or revealing more path detail than maintainers need.

15
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.800
Recovery also requires testing.

16
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:33.780
Exercise disk errors where practical, permission failures, interrupted operations, partial writes, cleanup failures, and restart cleanup.

17
00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:38.920
These paths are easy to neglect because successful temporary-file use often looks simple.

18
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.960
Operational hygiene turns temporary files from an afterthought into a managed lifecycle.

19
00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:51.380
The system should know where files are created, how they are bounded, how they are cleaned, and who responds when cleanup stops working.

20
00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:54.060
Cleanup policies should be conservative.

21
00:01:54.160 --> 00:02:04.480
Startup cleanup should target only application-owned directories and known naming patterns, and it should avoid sweeping broad shared locations where unrelated files may exist.
